    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 180457\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n957 PM PDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Virga and light showers are possible today and tomorrow.\n\n* A more robust trough will bring another round of increased\n  precipitation chances and gusty winds to the region towards the\n  middle of the week. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Friday.\n\nthrough Friday.\n\nAs the closed low over Southern California/Northern Baja begins to \nshift eastward, we will see precipitation chances begin to increase \nsouth and east of the I-15 corridor later this afternoon. This low \nwill eventually move inland overnight, becoming an open wave as it \nswings through on Sunday. This will drive additional isoalted-to-\nscattered showers around the Colorado River Valley as well as across \nMohave, Clark, and Lincoln Counties on Sunday. Precipitation impacts \nfrom these showers will be minimal (e.g., sprinkles ruining a fresh \ncar wash) as rain will have to compete with the dry low-levels of \nthe atmosphere in order to make it to the ground. Aside from light, \nisolated showers, weather across the region will remain relatively \nmild with seasonable temperatures and relatively light winds. \n\nAs this system lifts out of the region on Monday, focus shifts to \nthe trough digging along the California Coast. As this trough \napproaches the region, breezy southerly to southwesterly winds will \npick up across Inyo County and the southeastern Great Basin on \nTuesday, spreading across the Mojave Desert on Wednesday. In \naddition to gusty winds, this system will also bring increased \nprecipitation chances to the region beginning as early as Tuesday \nand continuing as late as Friday. Uncertainty regarding how quickly \nthis trough will move through the area continues to result in \nuncertainty regarding the duration of precipitation chances as the \nGFS ensemble mean solution favors a quicker moving trough than its \nECMWF counterpart. In addition to discrepancies regarding how \nquickly this system moves through the area, there remain \ndiscrepancies regarding how much moisture will be present, which \ntranslates to uncertainty regarding precipitation amounts. Cooler \ntemperatures will also accompany this trough, with cooler than \nnormal temperatures expected during the second half of the week. \n&&\n\n\n \n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Light \nwinds less than 8 knots will continue through Sunday. While an \nisolated light shower will be possible in the vicinity of the field \nthrough 08Z, VCSH has been removed for the overnight period given \nthe reduction in shower coverage observed over the past few hours. \nMid- and high-level clouds will remain over the area through the \nmorning, followed by gradually decreasing coverage during the \nafternoon and evening. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Scattered light showers \ncontinue over northwest Arizona and far eastern southern Nevada late \nthis evening. This activity will continue into Sunday morning before \ngradually weakening and moving east of the region. While an isolated \nshower or two will be possible near the Colorado River Valley TAF \nsites through the night, VCSH has been removed from the forecast \ngiven the low shower coverage currently being observed. Winds will \nremain light overnight and Sunday, with speeds generally remaining 8 \nknots or less in all areas. Mid- and high-level clouds will remain \nover the eastern third of the region through the morning, followed \nby gradually decreasing coverage during the afternoon and evening. \n\n\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Stessman\nAVIATION...Murdock\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
